  "live_records": {
    "q-99288": {
      "dom_id": "step-q99288",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that $p(x)=2x$ and $q(x)=7{x}^{4},$ what is ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x))?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2x\\cdot 7{x}^{4}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }((2\\cdot 7){x}^{1+4}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(14\\cdot {x}^{5}) \\\\  & =14\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{5}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $14$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(14{x}^{5})=\\infty .\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
