    "q-302063": {
      "dom_id": "step-q302063",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "If $p(x)=3{x}^{6}$ and $q(x)=4x,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{{x}^{5}\\cdot q(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's calculate ${x}^{5}\\cdot q(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{5}\\cdot q(x)={x}^{5}\\cdot (4x)=4{x}^{6}.\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions $p(x)$ and ${x}^{5}\\cdot q(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{{x}^{5}\\cdot q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{6}}{4{x}^{6}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
