      "problem": "If $r(x)=-2{x}^{8}$ and $s(x)=\\frac{11}{{x}^{10}}$, then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)\\cdot s(x))=$  \n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot r(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (-2{x}^{8})=-2{x}^{10}\n$$\n\nWhen we multiply the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)$ and $s(x)$, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)\\cdot s(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-2{x}^{10}\\cdot \\frac{11}{{x}^{10}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-22 \\\\  & =-22\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
